    36	
    37	## Definition
    38	
    39	Let $A$ and $B$ be sets and let $f : A \to B$ be a function.
    40	
    41	- $f$ is **injective** (one-to-one) if $f(x) = f(y)$ implies $x = y$, for all
    42	  $x, y \in A$.
    43	- $f$ is **surjective** (onto) if for every $b \in B$ there is some $x \in A$
    44	  with $f(x) = b$; equivalently, the image $f[A] := \{ f(x) : x \in A \}$ equals
    45	  $B$.
    46	- $f$ is **bijective** if it is both injective and surjective.
    47	
    48	For $S \subseteq A$ we write $f[S] = \{ f(x) : x \in S \}$ for the image of $S$,
    49	and for $T \subseteq B$ we write $f^{-1}[T] = \{ x \in A : f(x) \in T \}$ for the
    50	preimage of $T$.
    51	
    52	## Remarks
    53	
    54	- **Composition.** If $f : A \to B$ and $g : B \to C$ are both injective then so
    55	  is $g \circ f$, since $g(f(x)) = g(f(y))$ forces $f(x) = f(y)$ and then
    56	  $x = y$; if both are surjective then so is $g \circ f$, since any $c \in C$ is
    57	  $g(b)$ for some $b \in B$ and that $b$ is $f(a)$ for some $a \in A$. Hence a
    58	  composition of bijections is a bijection. These one-line verifications are used
    59	  silently throughout the library.
    60	
    61	- **Inverses.** $f : A \to B$ is bijective exactly when there is a function
    62	  $f^{-1} : B \to A$ with $f^{-1}(f(x)) = x$ for all $x \in A$ and
    63	  $f(f^{-1}(b)) = b$ for all $b \in B$; that two-sided inverse is unique, and it
    64	  is itself a bijection. Injectivity alone gives a bijection from $A$ onto the
    65	  image $f[A]$, and hence an inverse defined on $f[A]$ only. No choice principle
    66	  is involved: the value $f^{-1}(b)$ is the *unique* $x$ with $f(x) = b$, so it
    67	  is determined rather than selected.
    68	
    69	- **What this item does and does not do.** Functions, ordered pairs, Cartesian
    70	  products, images and preimages are ambient ZFC vocabulary in this library, on
    71	  the same footing as "subset" and "union": a function $A \to B$ is a set of
    72	  ordered pairs, single valued and total on $A$. This item only fixes the three
    73	  adjectives and the notation used for them. Nothing here is proved, and nothing
    74	  here is assumed beyond the set-theoretic background already in use.

