    56	This item is bookkeeping, not mathematics: it records what each statement in the
    57	neighbourhood of the Axiom of Choice actually costs, so that later pages can
    58	state honestly which of their theorems are choice-free. Nothing here is proved
    59	that is not proved elsewhere in the library, and everything cited without a
    60	link is flagged as such.
    61	
    62	**Equivalent to the Axiom of Choice over ZF.**
    63	
    64	- **Zorn's lemma** and **the well-ordering theorem**. Both equivalences are
    65	  proved in this library, in [[cor-ac-iff-zorn]] and
    66	  [[cor-ac-iff-well-ordering]]. Each of *these two statements* costs exactly the
    67	  Axiom of Choice, no more and no less. A theorem proved *with* one of them costs
    68	  at most the Axiom of Choice, which is an upper bound and not a lower one: the
    69	  theorem may well follow from something strictly weaker, and the ultrafilter
    70	  lemma below is exactly that case.
    71	- **Tychonoff's theorem**, that a product of compact spaces is compact. The
    72	  implication from the Axiom of Choice is the familiar one; the converse is
    73	  Kelley 1950. Not proved here, and worth a warning when it is: Kelley's
    74	  original argument needs a repair, supplied by Schechter, and without the
    75	  repair it yields only the Boolean prime ideal theorem
    76	  ([[rem-schechter-kelley-tychonoff]]).
    77	- **Every vector space has a basis.** The implication from the Axiom of Choice
    78	  is a routine application of Zorn's lemma, and it **is** proved here, in
    79	  [[cor-every-vector-space-has-a-basis]] by way of
    80	  [[thm-every-independent-set-extends-to-a-basis]]. The converse is a hard
    81	  theorem of Blass, 1984, which is **not** proved here and is quoted on the
    82	  authority of the references. The equivalence itself is
    83	  recorded in the library, in [[rem-hahn-banach-hamel-basis-open]], where it
    84	  fixes the upper endpoint of an open question about the strength of
    85	  Hahn-Banach.
    86	- **Cardinal comparability**, that for any two sets one injects into the other.
    87	  This is Hartogs 1915, and the full equivalence with the Axiom of Choice **is**
    88	  now proved here, in [[thm-cardinal-comparability-iff-ac]], by way of the
    89	  construction of [[thm-hartogs]].
    90	
    91	**Strictly weaker than the Axiom of Choice.**
    92	
    93	Each of the following is a genuine choice principle: not provable in ZF
    94	(assuming ZF consistent), yet strictly weaker than the Axiom of Choice.
    95	
    96	- **The ultrafilter lemma**, that every filter extends to an ultrafilter,
    97	  equivalently the **Boolean prime ideal theorem**. The Axiom of Choice implies
    98	  it, that implication being the one thing here this library does prove; it is
    99	  not provable in ZF (Feferman 1965,
   100	  [[rem-feferman-no-free-ultrafilter-in-zf]]), and it does not imply the Axiom
   101	  of Choice (Halpern and Levy 1971, [[rem-halpern-levy-bpi-not-ac]]). Both of
   102	  those are external results, recorded and not proved here. The proof given here
   103	  ([[thm-ultrafilter-lemma]]) runs through Zorn's lemma, so it pays full price
   104	  for a statement that costs strictly less: exactly the overpayment set out in
   105	  [[rem-choice-strengths]], and the reason a cost may not be read off a proof.
   106	- **Dependent choice (DC)**, that if every element of a nonempty set $X$ stands
   107	  in a relation $R$ to some element of $X$, then for **every** $a \in X$ there is a
   108	  **sequence** $(x_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$ in $X$ with $x_0 = a$ and
   109	  $x_n \mathbin{R} x_{n+1}$ for every $n$ ([[def-dependent-choice]]). The
   110	  prescribed starting point belongs to the statement: deleting the clause
   111	  $x_0 = a$ gives a **formally weaker** principle, which that item records as an
   112	  immediate consequence and does not derive DC back from, so the two are not
   113	  interchangeable here. What DC delivers is an $\mathbb{N}$-indexed sequence, not a chain in this
   114	  library's sense ([[def-chain]], a totally ordered subset of a poset): $R$ need
   115	  not be an order at all, and the terms need not be distinct. Implied by the
   116	  Axiom of Choice, and implies countable choice; neither implication reverses,
   117	  which is a relative-consistency result and so holds under the standing
   118	  assumption that ZF is consistent. It
   119	  is the principle quietly used whenever a sequence is built by picking each term
   120	  in terms of the previous one. The two non-reversals are external results that
   121	  this library does not prove; it records them, with their sources, in
   122	  [[def-dependent-choice]].
   123	- **Countable choice ($\mathrm{AC}_\omega$)**, choice functions for countable
   124	  families. Implied by dependent choice, and still not a theorem of ZF: Cohen's
   125	  first model contains an infinite set of reals with no countably infinite subset
   126	  ([[rem-cohen-first-model]]), which is already a failure of
   127	  $\mathrm{AC}_\omega$.
   128	
   129	**These three are not ranked on a line, and none of them is "the weakest".** The
   130	only implications among them PROVABLE IN ZF are $\mathrm{DC} \Rightarrow \mathrm{AC}_\omega$
   131	and its consequences; the ultrafilter lemma is incomparable with dependent choice
   132	and with countable choice alike, neither implying nor implied by either. Every
   133	*non*-implication in that sentence is a relative-consistency result, quoted from
   134	the references and conditional on the consistency of ZF: what is established is
   135	that ZF, if consistent, does not prove the missing implications, never that they
   136	are outright false. Those
   137	incomparabilities are quoted from the references, not recorded here. So a theorem
   138	must be labelled with the principle it actually uses, never with a position on a
   139	scale, and a phrase like "the weakest of the three" is simply not available.
   140	
   141	**Choice-free, and deliberately so.**
   142	
   143	- **[[thm-hartogs]]**: for every set $A$ there is a least ordinal that does not
   144	  inject into $A$. This is the ZF substitute for cardinal comparability, and its
   145	  whole value is that it needs no choice.
   146	- **[[lem-well-order-comparability]]**: any two well-orders are comparable.
   147	  Comparability of arbitrary sets is equivalent to the Axiom of Choice;
   148	  comparability of well-orders is free.
   149	- Transfinite induction, transfinite recursion, the assignment of order types,
   150	  and the Burali-Forti theorem are all theorems of ZF. Transfinite recursion
   151	  spends Replacement, and that is the only axiom beyond the basic ones it needs;
   152	  the standard confusion on this point is recorded as
   153	  [[fs-transfinite-induction-needs-choice]].
   154	- Rigidity of well-orders ([[lem-well-order-rigid]]) is the structural reason for
   155	  all of this: the witnessing isomorphisms are unique, so they never have to be
   156	  chosen.
   157	
   158	**Where this library spends choice.**
   159	
   160	Full choice is spent at one step inside Zorn's lemma, and directly in some results
   161	that do not route through Zorn; more than one *result* assumes it, and there are a
   162	second and a third, weaker principle each assumed elsewhere. All four facts belong
   163	in the ledger.
   164	
   165	- **One step inside Zorn, and direct uses besides.** The Axiom of Choice is used at a single step of the
   166	  proof of Zorn's lemma ([[thm-zorn]]), to select a strict upper bound for every
   167	  chain at once; the fixed point theorem underlying it ([[thm-bourbaki-witt]]) is
   168	  choice-free. Most results in this library that assume full choice reach it
   169	  through that step, but not all: some apply the Axiom of Choice directly instead,
   170	  for example [[thm-product-universal-property]], which uses it to obtain a point
   171	  of an arbitrary product of nonempty sets without routing through Zorn's lemma.
   172	- **The results that assume full choice.** [[thm-zorn]] itself is the first of
   173	  them: its statement takes the Axiom of Choice as a standing hypothesis, which
   174	  is why the step above lives inside it. On this page: the well-ordering
   175	  theorem ([[thm-well-ordering-theorem]]), which takes the Axiom of Choice as a
   176	  hypothesis; and the **cardinality assignment** of [[def-cardinal]], which
   177	  assumes it in order to well order an arbitrary set. That last one is easy to
   178	  miss, because the *property* of being a cardinal is choice-free and only the
   179	  attachment of $|X|$ to an arbitrary $X$ is not. The two equivalences
   180	  [[cor-ac-iff-zorn]] and [[cor-ac-iff-well-ordering]] do **not** belong in this
   181	  list: each is proved in ZF outright and assumes no choice principle, saying
   182	  only that the statements it names imply one another. Elsewhere in the library,
   183	  [[thm-ultrafilter-lemma]] is proved through Zorn's lemma and so also pays full
   184	  price, although its statement costs strictly less; and the **Hausdorff maximal
   185	  principle**, that every poset has a maximal chain, is drawn from Zorn as a
   186	  consequence in [[ex-zorn-poset-of-chains]] and pays the same price, the
   187	  chain-completeness verified there being free.
   188	- **A weaker principle, spent separately.** [[thm-countable-union-of-countable]]
   189	  and [[thm-perfectly-normal-implies-completely-normal]] are each stated under
   190	  countable choice ([[def-countable-choice]]) and flag the one step that spends
   191	  it. That is **not** a use of the Axiom of Choice: $\mathrm{AC}_\omega$ is
   192	  strictly weaker, so neither theorem may be relabelled choice-free or lumped in
   193	  with the full-choice results above.
   194	- **A third principle below full AC.** [[thm-urysohn-lemma]] and
   195	  [[thm-tietze-extension-theorem]] are each stated under dependent choice
   196	  ([[def-dependent-choice]]), each applying dependent choice directly to a single
   197	  relation on stage-tagged states to build an $\mathbb{N}$-indexed sequence of
   198	  approximations; the standalone stagewise form
   199	  [[lem-dependent-choice-along-a-sequence-of-relations]] is a related construction
   200	  that neither theorem cites. DC implies $\mathrm{AC}_\omega$
   201	  and neither reverses (recorded above), so this is a third, distinct cost: not
   202	  the Axiom of Choice, and not interchangeable with the countable-choice results
   203	  either, even though DC happens to imply enough to reprove them.
   204	
   205	**What is not proved anywhere here.**
   206	
   207	The **independence** of the Axiom of Choice from ZF. Gödel's 1938 constructible
   208	universe shows ZF cannot refute it ([[rem-godel-constructible-universe]]);
   209	Cohen's 1963 forcing shows ZF cannot prove it
   210	([[rem-cohen-forcing-ac-independent]]). Both are external results requiring
   211	machinery this library does not yet contain, and both are conditional on the
   212	consistency of ZF. Every statement in
   213	the library that relies on them is written conditionally, as in
   214	[[fs-zorn-provable-in-zf]] and [[fs-every-set-well-orderable-in-zf]]. A reader
   215	who wants the unconditional version of those statements will not find it, here
   216	or anywhere.

